      "source": "Broadcom TechDocs, VCF 9.1: First VCF Instance FQDNs and IP Addresses; Additional VCF Instance FQDNs and IP Addresses; Network Deployment Options for VCF Components",
      "url": "https://techdocs.broadcom.com/us/en/vmware-cis/vcf/vcf-9-0-and-later/9-1/planning-and-preparation/vcf-components-fqdns-and-ip-addresses/first-vcf-instance-fqdns-and-ip-addresses.html",
      "verified": "2026-11-26",
      "notes": "Every VCF component needs a unique FQDN and a static IP with forward (A) and reverse (PTR) DNS; no capital letters in FQDNs; containerized services don't support .local domains. 'instance' rows are repeated for every VCF instance (the additional-instance page lists vCenter, ESX hosts, NSX Manager nodes and VIP, SDDC Manager, Cloud Proxy, management services instance components, services runtime, VCF Automation with 5 node IPs). 'fleet' rows are in the first instance only (VCF Operations, License Server, management services fleet components, log management, Ops for Networks: not on the additional-instance page). The Identity Broker is listed for additional instances as Day-N; the studio counts one, in the first instance. The first-instance page lists a services runtime FQDN under VCF Automation as well as under management services; both are kept for the first instance. Counts are the simple / HA columns. VCF Operations data node: HA with medium or large only. Edge node management IPs: static (RCMD-COMSAZ-016). Management VM traffic runs on the ESX management network by default; a dedicated VM management network is a supported option (Network Deployment Options page). The studio plans a dedicated VLAN for each.",
